# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Class 5 Ch 9 Coconut Farm: long-form descriptions of the 8 interactive tools, in the master prompt format
(sections A to D, headings 1 to 8). Two dicts keyed by the tool media id: DESCRIPTIONS and OBJECTIVES."""

DESCRIPTIONS = {
    "M1.S1.T1.C2.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Coconut Array\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M1.S1.T1.C2.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Exploration\n"
        "- Textbook source: Class 5 Ch 9 Coconut Farm, the opener (the array of coconuts and the Division Facts box). The story tray is 5 rows of 7; every later number is a practice value (developer only).\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student sees that one array of coconuts gives a multiplication fact and two division facts: split the coconuts into groups by the columns, or into groups by the rows.\n"
        "- Out of scope: the words dividend, divisor and quotient, remainders, dividing by bigger numbers, any method of long division.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A wooden packing-shed table in warm light, as a photo scene. A wide wooden coconut tray fills about 60% of the stage. It holds the story array: 5 rows of 7 real coconut cut-outs (brown husk, three dark eyes), each about a thumb-width across. Small labels sit on the tray edge: \"Rows 5\" and \"Columns 7\".\n"
        "- Under the tray are three guess tiles: \"12\", \"35\", \"57\". A soft pulsing ring on the tray invites a tap.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Susie laid out her coconuts. How many are there? Make a guess.\"\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- The tray has up to 9 rows and 9 columns of round holes. A coconut sits in a hole when it is added, and pops out when it is taken away. Everything is photo-style; the table is a photo of dark wood.\n"
        "- Rows stepper: a small round \"−\" and \"+\" on the left edge of the tray. Columns stepper: the same on the top edge. They are only active when an activity asks the child to build.\n"
        "- Two on-stage buttons under the tray: \"Groups by columns\" and \"Groups by rows\". They are hidden until Activity 3.\n"
        "- Fact board under the tray, three lines with empty slots: line 1 \"rows × columns = total\", line 2 and line 3 \"total ÷ number of groups = number in each group\". Below it, a strip of number cards (large wooden tiles). Dragging a card copies it; the strip never runs out.\n"
        "- Goal chips on the stage corner, for example \"4 rows of 7\". A small mute icon sits in the stage corner (soft sounds only).\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Tap \"+\" or \"−\" to add or remove one row or one column. The coconuts in all and the two counts update at once. At most 9 rows and 9 columns: at 9 the \"+\" greys out and says \"The tray holds 9 at most.\"\n"
        "- Tap \"Groups by columns\": the columns are outlined in 7 colours with a number badge on each. Tap \"Groups by rows\": the rows are outlined the same way. One button at a time; tapping the other replaces the outline.\n"
        "- Drag a number card into a slot of the fact board. A right card locks in with a soft chime. A wrong card slides back with a short clue.\n"
        "- \"Reset\" clears the tray and the board of the current activity; \"Hint\" opens the next hint level; \"Check\" confirms the board and then reads \"Next\". A guess tile is tapped once and the result plays.\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Susie laid out her coconuts. How many are there? Make a guess.\" Goal chip: \"Coconuts in all\". Setup: the story tray, 5 rows of 7, steppers locked, row and column labels shown. Does: taps a guess tile, then taps the tray. Guess: the tile (12, 35 or 57). Result: the rows light up one by one with a running total 7, 14, 21, 28, 35 and the strip says \"5 rows of 7 coconuts make 5 × 7 = 35 coconuts in all.\" Help: guided, labels and running total shown.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided). Goal line: \"Build 4 rows of 7 coconuts.\" Goal chip: \"4 rows of 7\". Setup: an empty tray, \"Rows 0\", \"Columns 0\". Does: taps the steppers until the tray has 4 rows of 7, then fills line 1 with the cards 4, 7 and 28 (strip: 4, 7, 28, 11, 47). Guess: before building, a tile \"11\", \"28\" or \"47\". Result: 28 coconuts and the strip says \"4 rows of 7 make 4 × 7 = 28 coconuts.\" Help: guided, the three slots are labelled \"rows\", \"columns\", \"in all\".\n"
        "- Activity 3 (guided). Goal line: \"Split the 28 coconuts into equal groups.\" Goal chip: \"Two ways to split\". Setup: the 4 rows of 7 tray, locked. Guess: a tile \"7 groups of 4\", \"4 groups of 7\" or \"28 groups of 1\" before pressing \"Groups by columns\". Does: presses \"Groups by columns\", then fills line 2 with 28, 7 and 4; presses \"Groups by rows\", then fills line 3 with 28, 4 and 7. Result: the strip says \"28 split into 7 groups has 4 in each group: 28 ÷ 7 = 4.\" then \"28 split into 4 groups has 7 in each group: 28 ÷ 4 = 7.\" Help: guided, each group shows its badge.\n"
        "- Activity 4 (independent). Goal line: \"Build 3 rows of 6 and write the three facts.\" Goal chip: \"3 facts\". Setup: empty tray, empty board, no labels on the slots. Guess: a tile \"3\", \"6\" or \"18\" for \"Which number is split in a division fact?\" Does: builds 3 rows of 6, fills the board with 3 × 6 = 18, 18 ÷ 6 = 3 and 18 ÷ 3 = 6. Then builds 2 rows of 9 and sees the total; before that, a tile \"More\", \"Less\" or \"Same\" for the total. Result: \"3 × 6 = 18 and 2 × 9 = 18: the same 18 coconuts in a different tray.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Activity 5 (independent). Goal line: \"Write the three facts for this tray.\" Goal chip: \"6 rows of 5\". Setup: a locked tray of 6 rows of 5 and an empty board. Guess: a tile \"5\", \"6\" or \"30\" for the number that comes first in a division fact. Does: fills the board. Result: \"30 coconuts are split, so 30 comes first: 30 ÷ 5 = 6 and 30 ÷ 6 = 5.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 (twist). Goal line: \"Build 1 row of 9. Write the facts.\" Goal chip: \"1 row of 9\". Guess: before pressing \"Groups by rows\", a tile \"1 group of 9\", \"9 groups of 1\" or \"2 groups\". Does: builds the tray, presses both Groups buttons, fills the board. Result: \"1 × 9 = 9. In 1 group there are 9: 9 ÷ 1 = 9. In 9 groups there is 1 each: 9 ÷ 9 = 1.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2 (twist). Goal line: \"Build 4 rows of 4. How many different division facts do you get?\" Goal chip: \"4 rows of 4\". Guess: a tile \"1\", \"2\" or \"3\". Does: builds the tray, presses both Groups buttons, fills the board. Result: \"4 groups of 4 both ways: 16 ÷ 4 = 4 is the same fact twice.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- Doing: the child builds an array, counts the total, then cuts the same coconuts into equal groups in two ways and reads each cut as a sentence.\n"
        "- Noticing: the total is the number that is split; the number of groups and the number in each group swap places between the two cuts; a different tray with the same total (3 × 6 and 2 × 9) gives different division facts.\n"
        "- In the child's words: \"One multiplication fact gives two division facts.\" \"The total goes first in a division fact.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce, the pill gets a tick, and the strip gives the exact success line of the activity. A missed guess is never marked wrong; the true result simply plays.\n"
        "- Mistake: adds rows and columns, for example 11 for 4 rows of 7 or 12 for 5 rows of 7 -> \"11 is 4 + 7. Count the coconuts in all: 4 rows of 7.\" The rows pulse one at a time.\n"
        "- Mistake: puts the total last, for example 7 ÷ 28 -> \"The total 28 is the number being split, so it comes first.\" The coconuts-in-all read-out pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: wrong number in a group slot, for example 7 in each group when there are 7 groups -> \"Look at one column. How many coconuts are in one group?\" One outlined group pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: the same division fact written twice on a tray that is not square -> \"That fact is already on the board. Press the other Groups button.\" The unused Groups button pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: presses \"Groups by columns\" with an empty tray -> \"Add a row and a column first.\" The steppers pulse.\n"
        "- Hints (button \"Hint\", three levels): (1) words: \"Count the rows. Then count the coconuts in one row.\" (2) a visual clue: the rows or the outlined groups pulse one at a time with their badges. (3) almost the answer: \"28 split into 7 groups has 4 in each group, so 28 ÷ 7 = 4. Now try the rows.\" (the numbers of the current tray)\n"
        "- Edge cases: 1 row of 9 makes \"9 ÷ 1 = 9\"; a square tray gives one fact twice; a \"+\" after 9 is refused with the message above; \"−\" on an empty tray does nothing.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 is done when the board holds 1 × 9 = 9, 9 ÷ 1 = 9 and 9 ÷ 9 = 1. Star Challenge 2 is done when the board holds 4 × 4 = 16 and 16 ÷ 4 = 4 on both division lines.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Split the coconuts by the columns or by the rows.\" \"One multiplication fact gives two division facts.\" Think and talk: \"Can a tray give only one division fact? When?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: packing-shed table (dark wood photo, warm light); wooden coconut tray with 9 by 9 round holes (photo cut-out); coconut cut-out (brown husk, three eyes, three-quarter view, with a soft shadow); wooden number cards 0 to 99 (code drawn); stepper buttons and group outlines (code drawn).\n"
        "- Language: English, short sentences, numbers as digits. Sounds: soft chime for a right card, soft knock for a wrong card; the mute icon turns them off.\n"
    ),
    "M1.S7.T7.C20.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Place Value Shifter\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M1.S7.T7.C20.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: Class 5 Ch 9 Coconut Farm, Patterns in Division and Place Value: Let Us Do Q1 to Q5 and the two place value charts, with the book's hints. Numbers in Activities 1 to 4 are practice values (developer only).\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student divides tens, hundreds and thousands with a table fact, and sees that one more zero in the number gives one more zero in the answer and that dividing by 10 slides every digit one place right.\n"
        "- Out of scope: remainders, long division, divisors that are not a table fact with zeros.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A wooden place value board on the packing-shed wall (painted planks, photo): four columns, Thousands (green), Hundreds (orange), Tens (blue) and Ones (pink), with large wooden digit tiles. The row \"Number\" shows 2, 8, 0; the row \"Answer\" is empty.\n"
        "- Below: a heap of 28 small wooden crates (photo cut-outs, ten coconuts peeping out, a \"10\" tag), 4 woven baskets, and a strip of digit tiles 0 to 9.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Share 280 coconuts among 4 baskets. Make a guess.\"\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- A crate holds 10 coconuts (a ten); a pallet of 10 crates with a \"100\" tag is a hundred. 2 to 9 baskets, each with a crate counter.\n"
        "- Digit tiles are about 1.2 thumb-widths wide. Under the Number row a rail with a round handle (Activities 3 and 4) ends in a small cart-shaped drop bin; a badge \"×10\" or \"×100\" shows how far a row has moved.\n"
        "- Goal chip, for example \"280 ÷ 4\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Crate mode: tap the heap and one crate or pallet goes into every basket, one round per tap; tap the last basket to take back a round.\n"
        "- Slide mode: drag the handle one place right per step; all tiles move together; a 0 leaving the Ones column falls into the bin (only a 0 may fall).\n"
        "- Match mode: drag the divisor's tiles left until they match a ghost of the number.\n"
        "- Answer: tap an Answer cell, then a digit tile; tap a filled cell to clear it. At most 4 digits; empty cells in front stay empty. \"Check\" confirms a row and then reads \"Next\"; \"Reset\" restores the activity; \"Hint\" opens the next hint level. Star cards show the book's wording; here only numbers and answers are listed.\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Share 280 coconuts among 4 baskets.\" Chip: \"280 ÷ 4\". Guess: each basket gets \"7\", \"70\" or \"700\". Does: taps the heap 7 times, writes 7 and 0. Result: \"28 tens shared among 4 baskets is 7 tens each. 28 ÷ 4 = 7, so 280 ÷ 4 = 70.\" Help: guided, the heap says \"28 tens\".\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided). Goal line: \"Share 2400 coconuts among 8 baskets.\" Chip: \"2400 ÷ 8\". Setup: 24 pallets. Guess: \"3\", \"30\" or \"300\". Does: taps 3 times, writes 3, 0, 0. Result: \"24 hundreds shared among 8 baskets is 3 hundreds each. 24 ÷ 8 = 3, so 2400 ÷ 8 = 300.\" Help: guided.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (guided). Goal line: \"Divide 6000 by 10. Slide the digits.\" Chip: \"6000 ÷ 10\". Setup: tiles 6, 0, 0, 0. Guess: where the 6 lands, \"Hundreds\", \"Tens\" or \"Ones\". Does: slides one place; then the row resets to 6000 for \"Now divide 6000 by 100.\" with a new guess and two places. Result: \"6000 ÷ 10 = 600: the 6 moved from the thousands to the hundreds.\" \"6000 ÷ 100 = 60: the 6 moved two places, to the tens.\" Help: guided, arrows between the columns.\n"
        "- Activity 4 (independent). Goal line: \"How many 91s are in 9100? And in 910?\" Chip: \"91 and zeros\". Setup: tiles 9 and 1 in Tens and Ones and a ghost 9100. Guess: \"10\", \"100\" or \"1000\". Does: slides the 91 left, zero tiles filling in behind, to match, writes the answer, then the same for 910. Result: \"9100 is 91 with two zeros added, so 9100 ÷ 91 = 100.\" \"910 is 91 with one zero added, so 910 ÷ 91 = 10.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 to 5 are independent: no labels, only the Hint button.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 (Let Us Do Q2, \"Solve the following division problems. Notice the patterns and discuss in class.\"). Chip: \"Nine divisions\". Nine shell chips: (a) 150 ÷ 3, (b) 80 ÷ 4, (c) 500 ÷ 5, (d) 100 ÷ 10, (e) 300 ÷ 100, (f) 500 ÷ 50, (g) 200 ÷ 20, (h) 440 ÷ 44, (i) 630 ÷ 63. The book's hints sit on three chips: \"How many 3s are in 150?\", \"5 × ___ = 500\", \"44 × ___ = 440\". Guess: how many answers are exactly 10, \"1\", \"5\" or \"9\". Answers: 50, 20, 100, 10, 3, 10, 10, 10, 10. Result: \"Five answers are 10: the number is the divisor with a zero added.\"\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2 (\"Patterns in Division and Place Value. Solve.\"). Chip: \"Nine more\". Chips: (a) 1000 ÷ 10, (b) 1000 ÷ 100, (c) 1600 ÷ 4, (d) 2000 ÷ 2, (e) 2000 ÷ 20, (f) 3700 ÷ 37, (g) 3300 ÷ 3, (h) 3300 ÷ 300, (i) 4000 ÷ 40; hints \"10 × ___ = 1000\" and \"37 × ___ = 3700\". Guess: how many answers are exactly 100, \"1\", \"4\" or \"9\". Answers: 100, 10, 400, 1000, 100, 100, 1100, 11, 100. Result: \"1000 ÷ 10 = 100 but 1000 ÷ 100 = 10: one more zero in the divisor, one less zero in the answer.\"\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3 (\"Now fill the place value chart.\"). Chip: \"Two charts\". Chart 1 shows 40 ÷ 10, 400 ÷ 10 and 4000 ÷ 10 filled (4; 4, 0; 4, 0, 0) and asks (a) 700 ÷ 70, (b) 1400 ÷ 100, (c) 220 ÷ 20, (d) 2200 ÷ 20. Chart 2 asks (a) 110 ÷ 11, (b) 860 ÷ 86, (c) 7500 ÷ 750, (d) 8800 ÷ 88, (e) 2400 ÷ 24, (f) 440 ÷ 22. Guess: how many of the ten answers start with the digit 1, \"3\", \"9\" or \"10\". Does: taps a cell, then a digit. Answers: chart 1: 10, 14, 11, 110; chart 2: 10, 10, 10, 100, 100, 20.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 4 (four story cards). Chip: \"Four stories\". (1) \"Sabina cycles 160 km in 20 days and the same distance each day. How many kilometres does she cycle each day?\" 8. (2) \"How many notes of ₹100 does Seema need to carry if she wants to buy coconuts worth ₹4200?\" 42. (3) \"The owner of an electric store has decided to distribute ₹5500 equally amongst 5 of his employees as a Diwali gift. What amount will each employee get?\" ₹1100. (4) \"What will happen if he distributes the same amount of money among 10 employees? Will each employee get more or less? How much money would he have to distribute if everyone must get the same amount as earlier?\" Less; ₹550 each; ₹11000. Guess: the sign to use, \"÷\", \"×\" or \"−\"; for card 4 first \"More\", \"Less\" or \"Same\". Answers go in a plain digit box (up to 5 digits).\n"
        "- Star Challenge 5. Chip: \"Two puzzles\". (a) \"Place the numbers 1 to 8 in the following boxes so that all the four operations, division, multiplication, addition and subtraction are correct. No number must be repeated.\" Eight hexagons: top row A ÷ B = C, A − D = F down the left, C × E = H down the right, F + G = H along the bottom; the child drags tiles 1 to 8 in. Guess: how many answers, \"1\", \"2\" or \"Many\". Answers: 6 ÷ 3 = 2, 6 − 5 = 1, 2 × 4 = 8, 1 + 7 = 8; or 8 ÷ 4 = 2, 8 − 7 = 1, 2 × 3 = 6, 1 + 5 = 6. After the first answer the card asks \"Is there more than one answer?\" and the child finds the second. (b) \"Fill in the blanks.\" Eight chips with one box each: the box ÷ 18 equals 100; the box ÷ 10 equals 610; the box ÷ 100 equals 72, 10 and 70; 870 ÷ the box equals 87; 200 ÷ the box equals 2; 130 ÷ the box equals 13. Answers: 1800, 6100, 7200, 1000, 10, 7000, 100, 10.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- The child shares tens and hundreds as crates and pallets, slides digits to see zeros move, and matches a divisor with zeros to a number.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Use the table fact, then put back the tens or the hundreds.\" \"One more zero in the number gives one more zero in the answer.\" \"Dividing by 10 slides every digit one place right.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the cell and goal chip turn green with a gentle bounce; the strip gives the exact line of the activity. A missed guess is never marked wrong; the true result simply plays.\n"
        "- Mistake: tens forgotten, for example the answer 5 for 150 ÷ 3 -> \"15 tens shared among 3 is 5 tens: put the tens back, 50.\" The Tens column glows.\n"
        "- Mistake: wrong zeros, for example the answer 30 for 2400 ÷ 8 -> \"2400 is 24 hundreds, so each basket gets 3 hundreds: 300.\" The pallets pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: a 0 in front, for example 014 -> \"No 0 is needed in front.\" A 0 left out, for example 100 as 1 -> \"100 needs 0 in the Tens and Ones: a 0 holds a place.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: a hexagon row that is not true -> \"2 × 3 is not 8. Which sign row is not right yet?\" That row pulses; a used number cannot be placed twice.\n"
        "- Hints (button \"Hint\", three levels): (1) words: \"Look at the number. How many tens or hundreds is it?\" (2) a visual clue: the Tens or Hundreds column glows. (3) almost the answer: \"280 is 28 tens. 28 ÷ 4 = 7, so 70.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: 440 ÷ 44 and 630 ÷ 63 give 10; 300 ÷ 100 = 3 has one digit; 1100 holds zeros.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Each Star Challenge is done when all its chips, rows or cards are green; Star Challenge 5 needs both hexagon answers.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Use the table fact, then put back the tens or the hundreds.\" \"One more zero in the number gives one more zero in the answer.\" \"Dividing by 10 slides every digit one place right.\" Think and talk: \"What happens to the answer when the divisor gets one more zero?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: painted-plank wall board (photo); wooden digit tiles 0 to 9 (code drawn); crate with a \"10\" tag, pallet with a \"100\" tag, woven basket (photo cut-outs); drop bin (flat drawing); shell chips and hexagon cells (code drawn).\n"
        "- Language: English, short sentences, place names in words.\n"
    ),
    "M2.S10.T10.C44.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Smart Splitter\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M2.S10.T10.C44.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: Class 5 Ch 9 Coconut Farm, Mental Strategies for Division: Examples 2 to 4, the two open questions, Try It! 1 to 6 and Let Us Solve (a) to (h). Numbers in Activities 1 to 4 are practice values (developer only).\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student divides a big number in the head by cutting it into easy parts, by rounding up and taking the extra away, or by halving again and again, and checks the answer by multiplying.\n"
        "- Out of scope: remainders, long division, halving a number whose half is not whole.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A workbench in the packing shed (photo of scratched wood). A long pale plank of coconut wood lies across the stage with 3654 painted on it in large white digits. A small handsaw hangs on a rail above it, two empty hooks below wait for the pieces, and a wooden tag \"÷ 6\" hangs from a nail.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"3654 ÷ 6 looks hard. Cut the plank into two easy pieces. Make a guess first.\"\n"
        "- Three guess tiles: \"about 60\", \"about 600\", \"about 6000\". The child taps one first.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Plank: about 80% of the stage width, ruler ticks every 100 with finer ticks every 10. The saw slides on the rail and snaps to the nearest 10. A label under the saw shows the left piece, for example \"3600\".\n"
        "- Pieces: after a cut the plank splits into two planks that swing onto the hooks, each with its number and a small box under it for its answer. A wide answer slot sits under both hooks.\n"
        "- Minus mode: the plank grows a pale ghost extension to the next round number (for example 5000) and the saw slides along it; the part beyond the number is the extra, drawn as a small shaded piece.\n"
        "- Halving mode: a chain of wooden boxes joined by arrows, each arrow carrying a pair of scissors and the word \"Half\".\n"
        "- Mode tags \"Split +\", \"Round up −\" and \"Halve\" (round wooden discs on the stage) are shown only where the child must choose. Goal chips sit on the stage corner, for example \"3654 ÷ 6\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Drag the saw to cut. Tap a piece box or an answer box; a number pad of the keys 0 to 9 and a delete key opens over the lower stage. At most 4 digits.\n"
        "- A cut must leave two pieces. A piece box is accepted when the piece divides exactly by the divisor. In minus mode the child slides the saw to a round number and the extra is worked out by the tool.\n"
        "- In halving mode the child types the half of the number in the last box, and the next arrow appears. \"Reset\" puts the plank back; \"Hint\" opens the next hint level; \"Check\" confirms an answer and then reads \"Next\".\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"3654 ÷ 6 looks hard. Cut the plank into two easy pieces. Make a guess first.\" Goal chip: \"3654 ÷ 6\". Setup: plank 3654, divisor 6. Guess: the three tiles. Does: slides the saw to 3600, types 600 and 9, then types 609. Result: \"3600 ÷ 6 = 600 and 54 ÷ 6 = 9, so 600 + 9 = 609.\" Then \"Check: 6 × 609 = 3654.\" Help: guided, each piece shows its table fact, \"36 ÷ 6 = 6\" and \"54 ÷ 6 = 9\".\n"
        "- Activity 2 (independent). Goal line: \"Cut 1484 into two easy pieces and divide.\" Goal chip: \"1484 ÷ 7\". Guess: \"about 20\", \"about 200\" or \"about 2000\". Does: cuts at 1400, types 200 and 12, then 212. Result: \"1400 ÷ 7 = 200 and 84 ÷ 7 = 12, so 200 + 12 = 212.\" Then \"Check: 7 × 212 = 1484.\" Help: independent, no table facts shown.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (guided). Goal line: \"4995 is just below a round number. Use the round number.\" Goal chip: \"4995 ÷ 5\". Setup: tag \"Round up −\" fixed. Guess: \"about 100\", \"about 1000\" or \"about 10000\". Does: slides the saw to 5000, sees the extra 5, types 1000 and 1, then 999. Result: \"5000 ÷ 5 = 1000 and 5 ÷ 5 = 1, so 1000 − 1 = 999.\" Then \"Check: 5 × 999 = 4995.\" Help: guided, the extra piece is labelled \"5 too many\".\n"
        "- Activity 4 (guided, then independent). Goal line: \"Halve again and again.\" Goal chip: \"392 ÷ 4\". Guess: how many halves for ÷ 4, \"1\", \"2\" or \"4\". Does: types 196, then 98. Result: \"Half of 392 is 196 and half of 196 is 98, so 392 ÷ 4 = 98.\" Then \"Check: 4 × 98 = 392.\" Second part, independent, chip \"520 ÷ 8\", guess \"2\", \"3\" or \"8\" halves: 260, 130, 65, \"520 ÷ 8 = 65\". Then \"Check: 8 × 65 = 520.\"\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 to 5 are independent: no labels, only the Hint button.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 (book Examples 2 to 4). Goal line: \"Observe the division carefully.\" Goal chip: \"Three ways\". Items: \"1248 ÷ 6. I can split the number into convenient parts.\" \"1992 ÷ 4. I can do it in a different way.\" \"128 ÷ 4. To divide by 4, I can halve twice.\" Guess: the child taps the mode tag they think is best for each. Does: works each one. Result: 1200 + 48 gives 200 + 8 = 208; 2000 − 8 gives 500 − 2 = 498; 128 gives 64 and 32, so 128 ÷ 4 = 32.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2 (book Try It! 1 to 4). Goal line: \"Try It!\" Goal chip: \"Four splits\". Setup: the signs are fixed, plus for (1) 64 ÷ 4, (2) 265 ÷ 5 and (4) 4824 ÷ 24, minus for (3) 1560 ÷ 8. Guess: \"about 10\", \"about 100\" or \"about 1000\" for the first. Does: any cut with two exact pieces is accepted. Result: for example 40 + 24 gives 10 + 6 = 16; 250 + 15 gives 50 + 3 = 53; 1600 − 40 gives 200 − 5 = 195; 4800 + 24 gives 200 + 1 = 201. The strip says \"Other splits may also be right.\"\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3 (book Try It! 5 and 6). Goal line: \"Try It! Halve again.\" Goal chip: \"Two chains\". Setup: (5) 168 ÷ 8 with the boxes \"Halve 168\", \"Halve ___\", \"Halve ___\"; (6) 144 ÷ 4 with \"Halve 144\" and \"Halve ___\". Guess: for (5), \"1\", \"2\" or \"3\" halves. Result: 84, 42, 21, so 168 ÷ 8 = 21; 72, 36, so 144 ÷ 4 = 36.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 4 (book Let Us Solve). Goal line: \"Solve the following problems using strategies used in the previous question.\" Goal chip: \"Eight divisions\". Setup: eight chips (a) 256 ÷ 4, (b) 545 ÷ 5, (c) 147 ÷ 7, (d) 1212 ÷ 6, (e) 648 ÷ 12, (f) 9648 ÷ 48, (g) 775 ÷ 25, (h) 796 ÷ 4 and the three mode tags. Guess: tap the chips that can be done by halving twice (a and h). Does: picks a tag for each chip and works it. Result: 64, 109, 21, 202, 54, 201, 31, 199.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 5 (book open questions). Goal line: \"Can you give 5 such examples where you can split the number conveniently?\" Goal chip: \"5 examples\". The child picks a divisor chip 2 to 9, sets a 3 or 4 digit number with digit arrows, cuts it and divides both pieces. Guess: before cutting, \"Will your divisor divide your number exactly?\", \"Yes\" or \"No\". Each exact example is saved as a coconut on a counter. Second part: \"For which other divisors and dividend might this strategy of repeated halving work?\" The child taps the divisors that work among 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 16 (right: 2, 4, 8, 16), then runs one chain with 8 and one with 16, for example 96 ÷ 8 gives 48, 24, 12 and 240 ÷ 16 gives 120, 60, 30, 15. Result: \"Your answer may differ.\" The strip says \"Repeated halving works for 2, 4, 8 and 16.\"\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- The child cuts a number where both pieces are table facts with zeros, divides each piece, and joins the answers with the same sign as the cut. With a round number just above, the extra is divided too and taken away at the end. Halving twice divides by 4; three times divides by 8.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Cut the number into parts the divisor divides easily.\" \"Divide each part, then add or take away the answers.\" \"Halve twice for 4 and three times for 8.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the pieces, the answer slot and the goal chip turn green with a gentle bounce; the strip shows the exact line of the activity. A missed guess is never marked wrong; the true result simply plays.\n"
        "- Mistake: a hard cut, for example 3650 -> \"6 does not divide 3650 easily. Slide the saw to where both pieces are table facts of 6.\" The rail marks near 3600 pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: a wrong answer for a piece -> \"Check by multiplying: what times 6 makes 3600?\" The piece pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: adds when the number was rounded up, for example 1000 + 1 -> \"5000 is 5 more than 4995, so take the extra away: 1000 − 1.\" The extra piece pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: a wrong half -> \"Half means two equal parts. Share 392 into 2 equal parts.\" The box pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: wrong number of halves -> \"Dividing by 4 is halving twice, because 2 × 2 = 4.\" The arrows pulse.\n"
        "- Hints (button \"Hint\", three levels): (1) words: \"Is there a round number close to this one?\" (2) a visual clue: the saw rail shows a glowing line at the best cut. (3) almost the answer: \"Cut at 3600. 3600 ÷ 6 = 600 and 54 ÷ 6 = 9.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: a piece equal to the divisor, 24 ÷ 24 = 1; a cut at the start or the end is refused (\"A cut must make two pieces\"); 128 ÷ 4 can also be done by splitting, and the strip says so.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Each Star Challenge is done when all its items are green; Star Challenge 5 when 5 examples are saved and the divisors are tapped.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Cut the number into parts the divisor divides easily.\" \"Round up, divide, then take the extra away.\" \"Halve twice to divide by 4.\" Think and talk: \"When would you round up instead of cutting?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: scratched wooden workbench (photo); plank of coconut wood with ruler ticks (photo cut-out, code-drawn ticks and digits); handsaw (photo cut-out); iron hooks; wooden tag; wooden boxes for the halving chain; scissors icon (flat drawing); number pad keys (code drawn).\n"
        "- Language: English, short sentences, numbers as digits.\n"
    ),
    "M2.S15.T15.C64.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Take Away Groups\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M2.S15.T15.C64.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: Class 5 Ch 9 Coconut Farm, Susie's Farm in Kerala (Examples 5 to 7) and Let Us Learn to Divide (726 ÷ 4, 902 ÷ 16, N = D × Q + R), with the remainder questions Let Us Solve Q5 and Q4. Numbers in Activities 1 to 4 are practice values (developer only).\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student divides by taking away groups of the divisor, adds the groups to find the quotient, reads what is left as the remainder, and checks with N = D × Q + R.\n"
        "- Out of scope: place value regrouping, mental splitting.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- The packing-shed floor (photo). A heap of coconuts (cut-out, about 30% of the stage width) sits on the left, labelled \"456 left\". A burlap sack stamped \"6\" beside it is one group.\n"
        "- Under the heap is the group dial: round buttons \"+100\", \"+10\", \"+1\", the line \"Take ___ groups of 6\" and a green button \"Take away\".\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Take away groups of 6 until no coconuts are left. How many groups do you think there are?\" Guess tiles: \"about 7\", \"about 70\", \"about 700\". The child taps one, then builds a take.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- The heap shrinks in proportion to what is left; a sack with a badge, for example \"70 sacks\", drops into a row of bags.\n"
        "- The ledger runs along the lower stage, one line per take: \"6 × 70 = 420 and 456 − 420 = 36\", with the groups added at the top, for example \"70 + 6\", and a counter \"Left: 36\".\n"
        "- N = D × Q + R board: four boxes with the signs between them; N and D are filled, the child fills Q and R. Estimate bar (Activity 3): ranges \"less than 100\", \"between 100 and 200\", \"more than 200\". Goal chip, for example \"456 ÷ 6\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Build a take with \"+100\", \"+10\" and \"+1\", then tap \"Take away\". A \"clear\" arrow empties the take; \"Undo\" removes the last ledger line. A take of 0 groups, or one that costs more coconuts than are left, is refused: the heap shakes and the dial blinks.\n"
        "- When the heap is finished the child types Q and R on a number pad (keys 0 to 9, delete; at most 4 digits). \"Check\" confirms Q and R and then reads \"Next\"; \"Reset\" restores the heap. In the bag cards a row of 25-coconut bags fills to show the full bags and the extra bag. Star cards show the book's wording.\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Take away groups of 6 until no coconuts are left.\" Goal chip: \"456 ÷ 6\". Guess: the tiles \"about 7\", \"about 70\", \"about 700\" groups. Does: takes 70 groups, so 456 − 420 = 36 are left, takes 6 groups, types Q = 76 and R = 0. Result: \"70 + 6 = 76, so 456 ÷ 6 = 76. Check: 6 × 76 = 456.\" Help: guided, a preview \"6 × 70 = 420\" shows before each take.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided). Goal line: \"Take away groups of 7. Watch what is left.\" Goal chip: \"509 ÷ 7\". Guess: \"Will some coconuts be left over?\" \"Yes\" or \"No\". Does: tries 100 groups, but 7 × 100 = 700 is too big; takes 70 groups, so 509 − 490 = 19 are left; takes 2 groups, so 19 − 14 = 5 are left; types Q = 72 and R = 5. Result: \"5 is less than 7, so no more groups can be taken. 509 ÷ 7: quotient 72, remainder 5. Check: 509 = 7 × 72 + 5, and 5 is less than 7.\" Help: guided, the counter says \"too big\" or \"fewer than 7\".\n"
        "- Activity 3 (independent). Goal line: \"Estimate first. Then take away the biggest groups that fit.\" Goal chip: \"845 ÷ 5\". Guess: the estimate bar; the strip then shows 5 × 100 = 500 and 5 × 200 = 1000. Does: takes 100 groups, 845 − 500 = 345; 60 groups, 345 − 300 = 45; 9 groups, 45 − 45 = 0; types Q = 169 and R = 0. Result: \"100 + 60 + 9 = 169, so 845 ÷ 5 = 169. Check: 5 × 169 = 845.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Activity 4 (independent). Goal line: \"Take away groups of 12.\" Goal chip: \"372 ÷ 12\". Guess: \"about 3\", \"about 30\" or \"about 300\". Does: takes 30 groups, 12 × 30 = 360 and 372 − 360 = 12; takes 1 group, 12 − 12 = 0; types 31 and 0. Result: \"30 + 1 = 31, so 372 ÷ 12 = 31. Check: 12 × 31 = 372.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 to 5 are independent: only Hint helps.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 (book Example 5). Goal line: \"Susie and Sunitha have a large coconut farm and they have harvested 1,117 coconuts in April. They sold 582 coconuts equally to 6 regular customers. How many coconuts did each customer get?\" Goal chip: \"582 ÷ 6\". Guess: \"Less than 100 each\" or \"More than 100 each\". Does: takes small groups (Susie's way, 20, 20, 20, 20, 10, 7), then redoes it in 2 takes (Sunitha's way, 90 and 7). Result: \"Susie took 6 takes: 20 + 20 + 20 + 20 + 10 + 7 = 97. Sunitha took 2: 90 + 7 = 97.\"\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2 (book Examples 6 and 7). Goal line: \"Each bag can hold 25 coconuts. How many bags would be needed to pack 97 coconuts?\" Chip: \"97 ÷ 25\". Then \"They pack the remaining coconuts for drying and extracting oil. They can pack 25 coconuts in each bag. How many bags will they need to pack the remaining coconuts?\" with 1117 − 582 = 535 left, so 535 ÷ 25. Guess (\"Guess the number of bags needed\"): \"about 2 bags\", \"about 20 bags\" or \"about 200 bags\". Does: takes 3 groups, 97 − 75 = 22 left, types 4 bags; takes 20 groups, 535 − 500 = 35, and 1 group, 35 − 25 = 10, types 22 bags. Result: \"3 full bags hold 75 coconuts and the 22 left need one more bag: 4 bags.\" \"21 full bags and 10 left need one more bag: 22 bags.\"\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3 (book Let Us Learn to Divide). Chip: \"Blanks\". Goal line: \"726 ÷ 4. Take away groups of 4.\" The ledger shows the book's blanks: the quotient parts 100 + ___ + ___, take away ___ leaving 326, take away 320 leaving 6, take away ___ leaving 2. Does: takes 100, 80 and 1 groups; the blanks fill with 400, 80, 4 and 1. For \"Could we have written 200 here?\" the child tries 200 groups, is refused because 4 × 200 = 800 is more than 726, and taps \"No\". Then \"902 ÷ 16\": blank ___ + 6, take away 800 then 96; the child types 50 and answers \"Could we have multiplied 16 by a larger tens?\" with \"No\", because 16 × 60 = 960 is more than 902. Then \"Is 726 the same as 4 × 181? Yes/No.\" (No: 4 × 181 = 724, so 726 = 4 × 181 + 2, with 2 left over.) and \"Is 902 the same as 16 × 56? Yes/No.\" (No: 16 × 56 = 896, so 902 = 16 × 56 + 6, with 6 left over.) Guess: the Yes or No tile. Result: \"N = D × Q + R.\"\n"
        "- Star Challenge 4 (book Let Us Solve Q5). Goal line: \"Identify the remainder, if any. Check if N = D × Q + R.\" Chip: \"Six divisions\". Chips (a) 887 ÷ 3, (b) 283 ÷ 8, (c) 745 ÷ 5, (d) 767 ÷ 26, (e) 530 ÷ 41, (f) 888 ÷ 67. Guess: how many have no remainder, \"1\", \"3\" or \"6\". Result (r is remainder): 295 r 2; 35 r 3; 149 r 0; 29 r 13; 12 r 38; 13 r 17.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 5 (book Let Us Solve Q4). Chip: \"Six more\". Goal line: \"Solve the following and identify the remainder, if any. Check whether N = D × Q + R in each case.\" Chips (a) 9,045 ÷ 5, (b) 1,034 ÷ 4, (c) 2,504 ÷ 7, (d) 8,900 ÷ 15, (e) 9,876 ÷ 32, (f) 7,506 ÷ 24. Guess: which has the biggest quotient, \"(a)\", \"(c)\" or \"(e)\". Result: 1,809 r 0; 258 r 2; 357 r 5; 593 r 5; 308 r 20; 312 r 18.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- The child takes the biggest group that fits, again and again, until what is left is less than one group. The groups are the quotient; what is left is the remainder.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Take away the biggest group that fits.\" \"Add the groups you took.\" \"N = D × Q + R, and the remainder is less than the divisor.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the counter turns green with a gentle bounce; the strip shows the activity's line. A missed guess is never marked wrong; the true result plays.\n"
        "- Mistake: a take that is too big, for example 100 groups of 7 -> \"700 is more than the 509 coconuts left. Take a smaller group.\" The heap shakes.\n"
        "- Mistake: R not less than D, for example R = 8 for 7 -> \"The remainder must be less than 7. Can you take one more group?\" \"Left\" pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: wrong Q -> \"Add up the groups you took: 70 + 2.\" The ledger pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: wrong R -> \"7 × 72 = 504. How many more to reach 509?\" The R box pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: only the full bags -> \"The 22 coconuts left still need a bag.\"\n"
        "- Hints (button \"Hint\", three levels): (1) words: \"How many groups of 6 can you take away at once?\" (2) a visual clue: the biggest take that fits glows on the dial. (3) almost the answer: \"Take 70 groups: 456 − 420 = 36 are left. Then take 6.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: remainder 0 (745 ÷ 5); a remainder near the divisor (530 ÷ 41, 38 left).\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 is done when both ways give 97; 2 when 4 and 22 bags are right; 3 when all blanks and Yes or No answers are right; 4 and 5 when all six chips are green.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Take away the biggest group that fits.\" \"N = D × Q + R.\" \"The remainder is always less than the divisor.\" Think and talk: \"Why can the remainder never be as big as the divisor?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: packing-shed floor (photo); heap of coconuts, stamped burlap sack, burlap bags (photo cut-outs); ledger, dial and number pad (code drawn).\n"
        "- Language: English, short sentences, the chapter's words dividend, divisor, quotient and remainder. The book's question whether 726 equals 4 × 181 shows the equal sign on the stage; here it is written \"the same as\".\n"
    ),
    "M3.S22.T22.C96.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Regroup and Share\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M3.S22.T22.C96.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: Class 5 Ch 9 Coconut Farm, Division Using Place Value (Examples 10 to 13, the digits question), Let Us Divide (a) to (d), Let Us Do Q1 with the riddle, and Let Us Solve divisions 1 to 12. Numbers in Activities 1 to 4 are practice values (developer only).\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student divides by place value: shares the blocks of each place equally, regroups what is left into the next place, writes 0 where a place cannot be shared, and checks with N = D × Q + R.\n"
        "- Out of scope: estimating by rounding, mental splitting, dividends over 4 digits.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A sharing table in a bright kitchen (photo scene). A wooden tray on the left holds base ten blocks for 94: 9 blue rods (tens), each about a thumb long, and 4 pink unit cubes. Four white plates stand in a row on the right; a small remainder dish sits by the tray. Under the plates: a quotient strip with the cells Thousands, Hundreds, Tens and Ones, and the book's empty long-division record.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Share 94 candies among 4 plates. How many tens can each plate get? Make a guess.\" Guess tiles: \"1\", \"2\", \"3\".\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Blocks in the book's colours: thousand cube (green), hundred flat (orange), ten rod (blue), unit cube (pink), each with a soft shadow.\n"
        "- 2 to 9 plates in a row. For a two-digit divisor, one large plate shows \"1 of 12 equal parts\" with faded plates behind it and a badge \"× 12\". Each plate has a stepper \"each gets: 0\" with \"−\" and \"+\".\n"
        "- A round \"Regroup\" button on the tray. The record writes a line per place, for example \"9 tens: 4 × 2 = 8, 1 left\". Goal chips on the stage, for example \"94 ÷ 4\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Set what each plate gets (0 to 9), then tap the blocks of the biggest place: they slide onto the plates, one per plate per round. Only the biggest number that fits is accepted; 0 is accepted when no round fits.\n"
        "- \"Regroup\" breaks the left-over blocks of a place into 10 of the next place, which fly into it. The quotient strip writes each digit; a 0 in front is shown faint and is not part of the answer.\n"
        "- For remainder questions a board \"N = D × Q + R\" opens with N and D filled; the child types Q and R on a number pad (keys 0 to 9, delete; at most 4 digits). \"Check\" confirms an answer and then reads \"Next\"; \"Reset\" restores the blocks. Star cards show the book's question word for word; here only numbers and answers are listed.\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Share 94 among 4 plates. Regroup what is left.\" Chip: \"94 ÷ 4\". Guess: tiles for the tens each plate gets. Does: shares the tens, regroups the ten left into ones, shares the ones, types R. Result: \"9 tens shared among 4 plates is 2 tens each and 1 ten left. 14 ones: 3 each and 2 left. 94 ÷ 4: quotient 23, remainder 2. Check: 94 = 4 × 23 + 2, and 2 is less than 4.\" Help: guided, place labels and arrows.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (independent). Goal line: \"Share 245 among 5 plates.\" Chip: \"245 ÷ 5\". Guess: digits in the answer, \"1\", \"2\" or \"3\". Does: sets 0 for the hundreds, regroups, shares the tens, regroups, shares the ones. Result: \"2 hundreds cannot be shared among 5 plates, so write 0. 24 tens: 4 each, 4 left. 45 ones: 9 each. 245 ÷ 5 = 49. Check: 5 × 49 = 245.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (independent). Goal line: \"Share 618 among 6 plates.\" Chip: \"618 ÷ 6\". Guess: the tens digit, \"0\", \"1\" or \"3\". Does: shares the hundreds, sets 0 for the tens, regroups, shares the ones. Result: \"6 hundreds: 1 each. 1 ten cannot be shared among 6 plates, so write 0 tens. 18 ones: 3 each. 618 ÷ 6 = 103. Check: 6 × 103 = 618.\"\n"
        "- Activity 4 (independent). Goal line: \"Share 1596 among 12 equal parts.\" Chip: \"1596 ÷ 12\". Setup: the large plate with \"× 12\". Guess: digits in the answer, \"2\", \"3\" or \"4\". Does: sets 0 for the thousands, then regroups and shares hundreds, tens and ones in turn. Result: \"15 hundreds: 1 each, 3 left. 39 tens: 3 each, 3 left. 36 ones: 3 each. 1596 ÷ 12 = 133. Check: 12 × 133 = 1596.\"\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 to 5 are independent: only the Hint button helps.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 (Examples 10 and 11): 62 ÷ 5, then 75 ÷ 8. Chip: \"62 ÷ 5\". Guess: the candies each child gets, \"6\", \"12\" or \"57\"; for 75 ÷ 8 \"Can we divide this into 8 equal parts without breaking them?\", \"Yes\" or \"No\". Result: 62 ÷ 5: 1 ten each and 1 left, 12 ones: 2 each and 2 left, so each child gets 12 candies and 2 are left. 75 ÷ 8: 7 tens cannot be shared (0), 75 ones: 9 each and 3 left.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2 (Examples 12 and 13 and the digits question): 324 ÷ 3, then 136 ÷ 6. Chip: \"324 and 136\". Guess: the tens digit of 324 ÷ 3, \"0\", \"1\" or \"2\". Result: 324 ÷ 3 = 108 (1 hundred each, 2 tens cannot be shared, 24 ones: 8 each); 136 ÷ 6: 1 hundred cannot be shared, 13 tens: 2 each, 1 left, 16 ones: 2 each, 4 left, quotient 22, remainder 4. Then the four divisions line up in a table and the book's digits question is asked; the child completes the sentence with tiles: \"If the first digit is [at least / less than] the divisor, the quotient has [as many digits as / one digit fewer than] the dividend.\"\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3 (Let Us Divide, four parts). Chip: \"Four divisions\". Each is done twice: the second way with blocks, the first way as a take-away record with blanks to fill. (a) 7,032 ÷ 6: digits 1, 1, 7, 2; blanks 100, 2, 6,000, 420, 12; quotient 1,172. (b) 3,005 ÷ 5: digits 0, 6, 0, 1; 600 + 1; take away 3,000, then 5; quotient 601; for \"Discuss why we have to write this 0 here\" the child taps \"The 0 holds the tens place.\" among three sentences. (c) 2,874 ÷ 14: 200 + 5; take away 2,800 (74 left), then 70 (4 left); digits 0, 2, 0, 5; remainder 4. (d) 9,805 ÷ 32: 300 + 6; take away 9,600 (205 left), then 192 (13 left); digits 0, 3, 0, 6; remainder 13. Guess: for (a) the digits in the answer, \"3\", \"4\" or \"5\". Result: \"Both ways give 1,172.\" and so on.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 4 (Let Us Do Q1 and the riddle). Chip: \"Missing numbers\". Ten boxes written divisor ) dividend ( quotient, ☐ for a missing digit: (a) 4 ) 480 ( ☐ ☐ 0; (b) 3 ) 906 ( ☐ 0 ☐; (c) 20 ) 400 ( ☐ 0; (d) 50 ) 100☐ ( ☐ 0; (e) ☐ ) 8☐8 ( 2 1 ☐; (f) 3 ) ☐36 ( 3 ☐ ☐; (g) ☐ ) 88 ( ☐ ☐; (h) ☐ ) 9☐0 ( ☐ ☐ ☐; (i) ☐ ) 180 ( ☐ ☐ ☐; (j) ☐ ) 6,480 ( ☐ ☐ ☐. A missing divisor is one digit; any digits with divisor × quotient equal to the dividend are accepted. Answers: (a) 120; (b) 302; (c) 20; (d) 20; (e) 212 or 217; (f) 312; (g) 88, 44, 22 or 11; (h) many; (i) 180; (j) 810 or 720. Then the riddle, answer 210. Guess: \"about 20\", \"about 200\" or \"about 2000\".\n"
        "- Star Challenge 5 (Let Us Solve, twelve divisions). Chip: \"Twelve divisions\". Chips: 1. 506 ÷ 5, 2. 918 ÷ 8, 3. 8,126 ÷ 7, 4. 9,324 ÷ 4, 5. 876 ÷ 6, 6. 7,008 ÷ 3, 7. 934 ÷ 12, 8. 829 ÷ 23, 9. 705 ÷ 18, 10. 8,704 ÷ 32, 11. 6,790 ÷ 45, 12. 5,074 ÷ 21. Blocks are optional. Guess: how many have no remainder, \"2\", \"4\" or \"8\". Answers (r is remainder): 101 r 1; 114 r 6; 1,160 r 6; 2,331 r 0; 146 r 0; 2,336 r 0; 77 r 10; 36 r 1; 39 r 3; 272 r 0; 150 r 40; 241 r 13.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- The child shares one place at a time, regroups what is left into the next place and shares again. A place that cannot be shared leaves a 0 in the answer. The first digit of the dividend against the divisor tells how many digits the answer has.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Share the biggest place, then regroup what is left.\" \"Write 0 where a place cannot be shared.\" \"Check: N = D × Q + R.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: plates, digit and goal chip turn green with a bounce; the strip gives the activity's line. A missed guess is never marked wrong; the true result simply plays.\n"
        "- Mistake: too many, for example 3 tens each for 4 plates and 9 tens -> \"4 × 3 = 12 is more than 9 tens. Try less.\" The plates pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: shares ones before regrouping -> \"1 ten is left. Regroup it into ones before you share the ones.\" The left-over rod pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: skips a 0 -> \"1 ten cannot be shared among 6 plates. Each plate gets 0 tens: write the 0.\" The Tens cell pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: R not less than D -> \"The remainder must be less than the divisor. Share once more.\" The remainder dish pulses.\n"
        "- Hints (button \"Hint\", three levels): (1) words: \"Look at the biggest place. Can every plate get at least one block?\" (2) a visual clue: the blocks of the current place glow. (3) almost the answer: \"9 tens among 4 plates: 2 tens each, 1 ten left. Regroup the ten into 10 ones.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: a 0 in the middle (103, 306); a faint 0 in front; remainder 0; repeated digits (133); (g) and (j) with more than one right answer.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Each Star Challenge is done when all its divisions, boxes or chips are green (Star Challenge 2 also needs the sentence).\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Share each place, then regroup what is left.\" \"Write 0 where a place cannot be shared.\" \"Check with N = D × Q + R.\" Think and talk: \"Why must we write 0 in 103?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: kitchen sharing table (photo); thousand cube, hundred flat, ten rod, unit cube, white plates, remainder dish (photo cut-outs); record lines and number pad (code drawn).\n"
        "- Language: English, short sentences, place names in words, \"candies\" in the stories as the book says.\n"
    ),
    "M3.S32.T32.C150.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Quotient Change\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M3.S32.T32.C150.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: Class 5 Ch 9 Coconut Farm, Let Us Solve Q5 parts A, B and C (340 ÷ 34, 192 ÷ 4, 352 ÷ 11 and their related divisions). Numbers in Activities 1 to 4 are practice values (developer only).\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student predicts how the quotient changes when the dividend or the divisor is doubled or halved, or both, and uses a known division to find related divisions.\n"
        "- Out of scope: remainders, changes other than doubling, halving and the book's times 4, divisions that do not give whole answers.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A coconut plot seen from above (photo of green grass with rows of palm tops), fenced by a brown rope rectangle that fills about 60% of the stage. The long side is labelled \"Quotient 30\" along the left edge, the short side \"Divisor 6\" along the bottom, and the middle of the plot \"Dividend 180\" in large white digits.\n"
        "- Four round buttons: on the middle label \"×2\" and \"÷2\" (they change the dividend), on the bottom side \"×2\" and \"÷2\" (they change the divisor). A row of three guess tiles sits under the plot: \"More\", \"Less\", \"Same\".\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"The plot is 180 ÷ 6 = 30. Double the 180. What happens to the quotient? Make a guess.\"\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- The long side is the quotient and the short side is the divisor. Sides grow or shrink in the same direction as their numbers but the plot always keeps a comfortable shape inside the stage; the labels carry the true numbers. Palm tops tile the plot.\n"
        "- A family strip of up to 6 small cards runs under the plot; each division reached is stored there, for example \"180 ÷ 6 = 30\". Goal chips on the stage, for example \"180 ÷ 6\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Tap one of the four round buttons: the sides stretch or shrink with a short animation and the new division appears with the quotient shown as \"?\".\n"
        "- Tap a guess tile before pressing a change button. Type the new quotient on a number pad (keys 0 to 9, delete; at most 4 digits) and tap \"Check\", which then reads \"Next\". \"Reset\" restores the plot.\n"
        "- A change that would not give a whole number (for example halving an odd number) is refused: \"That does not divide evenly.\"\n"
        "- In Star Challenges the plot is replaced by a card: the given division on the left, the division to solve on the right, and two sets of tiles, \"Dividend: same, ×2, ÷2, ×4\" and \"Divisor: same, ×2, ÷2, ×4\". The plot behind follows the child's choices.\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"The plot is 180 ÷ 6 = 30. Double the 180. What happens to the quotient? Make a guess.\" Goal chip: \"180 ÷ 6 = 30\". Guess: \"More\", \"Less\", \"Same\". Does: taps \"×2\" on the middle label, types 60. Result: \"180 doubled is 360 and 6 stays, so the quotient doubles: 360 ÷ 6 = 60. Check: 6 × 60 = 360.\" Help: guided, the sides are labelled and an arrow shows the stretch.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided). Goal line: \"Double the divisor. Keep the dividend.\" Goal chip: \"180 ÷ 6 = 30\". Guess: tiles. Does: taps \"×2\" on the bottom side, types 15. Result: \"6 doubled is 12 and 180 stays, so the quotient is halved: 180 ÷ 12 = 15. Check: 12 × 15 = 180.\" Help: guided.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (independent). Goal line: \"Halve the dividend, then halve the divisor.\" Goal chip: \"240 ÷ 8 = 30\". Guess: tiles before each. Does: \"÷2\" on the middle label, types 15; Reset; \"÷2\" on the bottom side, types 60. Result: \"240 halved is 120, 8 stays: the quotient is halved, 120 ÷ 8 = 15. 8 halved is 4, 240 stays: the quotient doubles, 240 ÷ 4 = 60.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Activity 4 (independent). Goal line: \"Change both numbers.\" Goal chip: \"120 ÷ 6 = 20\". Guess: for double both, \"More\", \"Less\", \"Same\"; for double the dividend and halve the divisor, \"Same\", \"Double\" or \"4 times\". Does: double both, types 20; then double the dividend and halve the divisor, types 80. Result: \"Both doubled: 240 ÷ 12 = 20, the quotient is the same.\" \"240 is double 120 and 3 is half of 6, so the quotient doubles twice: 240 ÷ 3 = 80.\"\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 to 3 are independent: the sides are not labelled, only the Hint button.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 (book Q5 part A). Chip: \"Part A\". Goal line: \"Find the solutions for part A. Observe the relations between the quotient, divisor and dividend and use it to answer parts B and C.\" Given: (a) 340 ÷ 34 = 10. To solve: (b) 340 ÷ 17, (c) 680 ÷ 17, (d) 680 ÷ 34, (e) 170 ÷ 17, (f) 680 ÷ 68. Guess: how many of the five have the quotient 10, \"0\", \"2\" or \"5\". Answers: 20, 40, 20, 10, 10.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2 (part B). Chip: \"Part B\". Given: (a) 192 ÷ 4 = 48. To solve: (b) 192 ÷ 8, (c) 384 ÷ 8, (d) 384 ÷ 4, (e) 384 ÷ 8, (f) 86 ÷ 2. Guess: which is bigger than 48, \"(b)\", \"(c)\" or \"(d)\". Answers: 24, 48, 96, 48, 43. Item (e) is the same division as (c) and the card says so. Item (f) is not made from 192 ÷ 4 by doubling or halving, so the tiles are locked and the card says \"Divide this one on its own.\"\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3 (part C). Chip: \"Part C\". Given: (a) 352 ÷ 11 = 32. To solve: (b) 704 ÷ 22, (c) 704 ÷ 11, (d) 352 ÷ 22, (e) 1,408 ÷ 44. Guess: how many have the quotient 32, \"1\", \"2\" or \"4\". Answers: 32, 64, 16, 32. Each Star Challenge keeps its given division and its answers in the family strip.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- The area is the dividend, one side is the divisor and the other is the quotient. If one side stays, the area and the other side change the same way. If the area stays and one side doubles, the other side is halved.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Double the dividend and the quotient doubles.\" \"Double the divisor and the quotient is halved.\" \"Double both and the quotient is the same.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the new division card, the goal chip and the pill turn green with a gentle bounce; the strip shows the exact line of the activity. A missed guess is never marked wrong; the true result simply plays.\n"
        "- Mistake: says the quotient doubles when the divisor doubles -> \"The divisor doubled, so each group is twice as big and there are half as many groups. The quotient is halved.\" The bottom side pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: says the quotient doubles when both double -> \"Both doubled, so the area and one side both doubled. The other side stays the same.\" Both sides pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: a wrong quotient -> \"Check by multiplying: 6 × ___ should be 360.\" The quotient side pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: wrong tiles in a Star Challenge -> \"Look at the numbers: 340 stays and 34 becomes 17. Which tile fits?\" The changed number pulses.\n"
        "- Hints (button \"Hint\", three levels): (1) words: \"Which number changed, the dividend or the divisor?\" (2) a visual clue: the changed side glows with an arrow for the stretch or the shrink. (3) almost the answer: \"The divisor doubled and the dividend stayed, so the quotient is halved: 30 becomes 15.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: both numbers change by the same amount, so the quotient is the same; times 4 on both in part C (e); the same division written twice in part B; one division that is not in the family, 86 ÷ 2.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 is done when all five divisions are green. Star Challenge 2 when the five are green and (f) is answered. Star Challenge 3 when all four are green.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Double the dividend and the quotient doubles.\" \"Double the divisor and the quotient is halved.\" \"Double both and the quotient is the same.\" Think and talk: \"If you double the dividend and halve the divisor, what happens to the quotient?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: aerial coconut plot (photo of green grass and palm tops, tileable); brown rope rectangle (code drawn); round buttons and family cards (code drawn); number pad keys (code drawn).\n"
        "- Language: English, short sentences. The words dividend, divisor and quotient are the chapter's own and are shown on the plot labels.\n"
    ),
    "M4.S34.T34.C158.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Equal Balance\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M4.S34.T34.C158.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: Class 5 Ch 9 Coconut Farm, Mathematical Statements: Q1 (five true or false statements) and Q2 (four statements to complete). Numbers in Activities 1 to 5 are practice values (developer only).\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student finds the value of both sides of an equal sign, decides if the statement is true or false, and completes a statement by making both sides the same.\n"
        "- Out of scope: statements with unknowns on both sides, always, sometimes or never true statements, brackets.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A brass two-pan balance on a market stall counter (photo, warm light, wooden stand). The beam is level. On the left pan sits a wooden card \"3 × 8\", on the right pan a card \"20 + 4\". Above each pan hangs an empty value tag \"?\". A small \"=\" plate sits on the middle of the beam.\n"
        "- Under the balance: three guess tiles \"Level\", \"Left side heavier\", \"Right side heavier\", and later the two answer tags \"True\" and \"False\".\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Does the balance stay level? Make a guess. Then find the value of each side.\"\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- The balance fills about 65% of the stage. Pans are about 20% of the stage width each; cards are cream wooden tiles with dark digits. Value tags are small brass labels above the pans.\n"
        "- A blank is an empty slot on a card, drawn as a dark square with a dotted border.\n"
        "- Statement chips (Star Challenges only) sit in a row above the balance, labelled (a), (b), and so on.\n"
        "- Goal chips on the stage, for example \"3 × 8 and 20 + 4\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Tap a value tag or a blank; a number pad (keys 0 to 9, delete; at most 3 digits) opens over the lower stage. When both value tags are filled the beam moves: level when the values are equal, tipping to the bigger side when they differ.\n"
        "- Tap \"True\" or \"False\" to give the answer after the beam has moved.\n"
        "- For a blank, the child types the missing number; the beam answers at once.\n"
        "- \"Reset\" restores the activity; \"Hint\" opens the next hint level; \"Check\" confirms an answer and then reads \"Next\".\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Find the value of each side.\" Goal chip: \"3 × 8 and 20 + 4\". Guess: \"Level\", \"Left side heavier\" or \"Right side heavier\". Does: types 24 on the left tag and 24 on the right tag, then taps \"True\". Result: the beam levels and glows green and the strip says \"3 × 8 = 24 and 20 + 4 = 24. Both sides are 24, so the statement is true.\" Help: guided, each card shows its steps.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided). Goal line: \"Find the value of each side.\" Goal chip: \"40 − 9 and 5 × 7\". Guess: the three tiles. Does: types 31 and 35, taps \"False\". Result: the beam tips to the right and the strip says \"40 − 9 = 31 and 5 × 7 = 35. The sides are different, so the statement is false.\" Help: guided.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (independent). Goal line: \"Is 8 + 5 the same as 6 + 7?\" Goal chip: \"8 + 5 and 6 + 7\". Guess: the three tiles. Does: types 13 and 13, taps \"True\". Result: \"8 + 5 = 13 and 6 + 7 = 13. Both sides are 13, so it is true. The equal sign does not mean that the answer comes next.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Activity 4 (guided). Goal line: \"Make both sides the same.\" Goal chip: \"5 × 8 = ___ + 12\". Guess: the missing number is \"Bigger than 12\" or \"Smaller than 12\". Does: types 40 on the left tag, then 28 in the blank. Result: the beam levels and the strip says \"5 × 8 = 40, so the blank is 40 − 12 = 28. Check: 28 + 12 = 40.\" Help: guided, the blank is outlined and the left tag is filled first.\n"
        "- Activity 5 (independent). Goal line: \"Make both sides the same.\" Goal chip: \"the blank ÷ 6 and 12 ÷ 2\". Guess: the missing number is \"Bigger than 6\" or \"Smaller than 6\". Does: types 6 for 12 ÷ 2, then 36 in the blank. Result: \"12 ÷ 2 = 6, so the blank divided by 6 must give 6. The blank is 6 × 6 = 36. Check: 36 ÷ 6 = 6.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 and 2 are independent: no steps shown, only the Hint button.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 (book Q1). Goal line: \"Find out whether the following statements are True (T) or False (F). A true sentence is one where both sides of the equal sign have the same value.\" Goal chip: \"Five statements\". Setup: chips (a) 8 × 9 = 70 + 2, (b) 20 − 6 and 7 × 3 on the two sides, (c) 48 ÷ 3 = 4 × 4, (d) 89 − 9 and 90 + 0 on the two sides, (e) 25 + 10 = 45 − 10; Guess: how many are true, \"1\", \"3\" or \"5\". Does: each chip is loaded on the balance, both values typed, True or False tapped. Result: (a) T, 72 and 72; (b) F, 14 and 21; (c) T, 16 and 16; (d) F, 80 and 90; (e) T, 35 and 35.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2 (book Q2). Goal line: \"Complete the following statements such that they are true.\" Goal chip: \"Four blanks\". Setup: chips (a) 7 × 6 = ___ + 17, (b) 87 + 6 = ___ × 31, (c) 63 + ___ = 74 − 4, (d) the blank ÷ 9 on one side and 16 ÷ 2 on the other. Guess: which blank is the biggest, \"(a)\", \"(c)\" or \"(d)\". Does: types the known side, then the blank. Result: (a) 25 because 42 − 17 = 25; (b) 3 because 93 ÷ 31 = 3; (c) 7 because 70 − 63 = 7; (d) 72 because 8 × 9 = 72.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- The child finds the value of each side separately, sees the beam level or tip, and learns that the equal sign means \"the same value on both sides\". For a blank, the child finds the value of the full side and then the number that makes the other side match.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Find the value of both sides.\" \"The same value is true, different values is false.\" \"A missing number makes the two sides the same.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the beam levels and glows green, the value tags and the goal chip turn green with a gentle bounce; the strip gives the exact line of the activity. A missed guess is never marked wrong; the true result simply plays.\n"
        "- Mistake: says False for 8 + 5 and 6 + 7 because 8 + 5 is not 6 -> \"The equal sign does not mean that the answer comes next. It means both sides have the same value. Find 6 + 7.\" The right pan pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: a wrong value, for example 49 for 40 − 9 -> \"Look at the sign: 40 − 9 takes 9 away.\" The sign pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: says True but the beam tips -> \"The sides are 31 and 35: different values, so it is false.\" The two tags pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: a wrong blank -> \"The two sides are not the same yet. 5 × 8 is 40. What number plus 12 makes 40?\" The beam tips and the blank pulses.\n"
        "- Hints (button \"Hint\", three levels): (1) words: \"What are the two sides of the equal sign?\" (2) a visual clue: the left pan glows and its card shows the steps of the sum. (3) almost the answer: \"3 × 8 = 24 and 20 + 4 = 24: the same value, so true.\" For a blank: \"5 × 8 = 40, so the blank is 40 − 12.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: adding 0 (90 + 0 is 90); a blank in the middle (63 + ___); a blank at the front (the blank ÷ 9); a blank that needs a division (93 ÷ 31); the same value on both sides in different forms (8 × 9 and 70 + 2).\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 is done when all five statements have the right tag. Star Challenge 2 is done when all four blanks level the beam.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Find the value of both sides.\" \"The same value on both sides makes a true statement.\" \"A missing number makes the two sides the same.\" Think and talk: \"Can you write a true statement with a sum on one side and a product on the other?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: market stall counter (photo); brass two-pan balance with a wooden stand (photo cut-out, beam and pans drawn separately so they can tilt); cream wooden cards; brass value tags (code drawn); number pad keys (code drawn).\n"
        "- Language: English, short sentences. On the stage the equal sign is shown as \"=\" between the sides; in this description the false statements are written in words so each side can be checked.\n"
    ),
    "M4.S36.T36.C164.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Always, Sometimes, Never\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M4.S36.T36.C164.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Puzzle\n"
        "- Textbook source: Class 5 Ch 9 Coconut Farm, Mathematical Statements: Q3 (a), (b), (c) and Q4 (five statements, the cells Always true, Sometimes true, Never true). Statements in Activities 1 to 4 are practice values (developer only).\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student tests a statement with several numbers and decides if it is always true, sometimes true or never true, knowing that one example that fails shows it is not always true.\n"
        "- Out of scope: proving a statement with letters or rules, numbers beyond 20 in the tests, answers below 0.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A market stall in warm light (photo). Three wicker baskets stand on the counter, each with a chalkboard tag: \"Always true\", \"Sometimes true\", \"Never true\". A wooden statement card is pinned above them: \"Adding 6 to an odd number gives an odd number.\"\n"
        "- Below the baskets a rack holds 20 coconuts, each with a number sticker from 1 to 20 (the even ones are greyed because this statement is about odd numbers). Under the card, an empty test table with the heads \"Number\", \"Result\", \"Works?\".\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Test the statement with some numbers. Make a guess first.\" Three guess tiles: \"Always\", \"Sometimes\", \"Never\".\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Baskets are about 18% of the stage width each. Coconut stickers are about a thumb wide. The statement card is a wooden plank about 60% of the stage width.\n"
        "- Test table: one row per test, for example \"3 + 6 = 9, odd, works\" with a green tick, or \"15 + 7 = 22, not less than 20, does not work\" with a red cross. Two counters above the table: \"Works\" and \"Does not work\".\n"
        "- For two-number statements, two empty slots \"A\" and \"B\" sit under the card; the child taps two coconuts. Goal chips on the stage, for example \"Adding 6 to an odd number\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Tap a coconut: the number is tested at once and a row is added to the table. Tap it again to remove the row. Numbers that do not fit the statement stay grey and say, when tapped, \"This statement is about odd numbers.\"\n"
        "- Drag the statement card into a basket to give the answer. The card is accepted only after at least 3 tests; before that it slides back with \"Test at least 3 numbers first.\"\n"
        "- \"Reset\" clears the table of this statement. \"Hint\" opens the next hint level. \"Check\" confirms the basket and then reads \"Next\".\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Test the statement with some numbers. Make a guess first.\" Goal chip: \"Adding 6 to an odd number\". Guess: \"Always\", \"Sometimes\", \"Never\". Does: taps 3, 7 and 11, then drags the card to \"Always true\". Result: \"3 + 6 = 9, 7 + 6 = 13 and 11 + 6 = 17 are all odd. None fails, so it is always true.\" Help: guided, each row says \"odd, works\".\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided). Goal line: \"Is it always, sometimes or never true?\" Goal chip: \"Subtracting 1 from an odd number\". Statement: \"Subtracting 1 from an odd number gives an odd number.\" Guess: the three tiles. Does: taps 3, 9 and 15, drags the card to \"Never true\". Result: \"3 − 1 = 2, 9 − 1 = 8 and 15 − 1 = 14 are all even. None works, so it is never true.\" Help: guided.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (independent). Statement: \"Adding 5 to a number gives an even number.\" Goal chip: \"Adding 5\". Guess: the three tiles. Does: taps 3 and 4, then one more, drags the card to \"Sometimes true\". Result: \"3 + 5 = 8 is even, but 4 + 5 = 9 is odd. Some work and some fail, so it is sometimes true.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Activity 4 (independent). Statement: \"Adding 7 to a number gives a number less than 20.\" Goal chip: \"Adding 7\". Guess: the three tiles. Does: taps 4, 9 and 15, drags the card to \"Sometimes true\". Result: \"4 + 7 = 11 and 9 + 7 = 16 are less than 20, but 15 + 7 = 22 is not. One example that fails is enough, so it is not always true. It is sometimes true.\" Help: independent. After 3 good tests the strip asks \"Can you find a number that fails? Try a big number.\"\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 and 2 are independent: no hints on the rack, only the Hint button.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 (book Q3). Goal line: \"Think about the following statements and find examples as suggested below.\" Goal chip: \"Three statements\". Guess: which one is never true, \"(a)\", \"(b)\" or \"(c)\". (a) \"When two odd numbers are added, the sum is even.\" \"Find 5 examples for the above statement. Can you find an example to show that the statement can be false?\" The child taps two odd coconuts for each of 5 examples (for example 1 + 3 = 4, 3 + 5 = 8, 7 + 9 = 16, 5 + 11 = 16, 9 + 9 = 18), then tries to make it false; none fails. Basket: Always true. (b) \"Multiplying a number by 2 can give an odd number.\" \"Give some example for this statement. Can you find any?\" Examples such as 2 × 3 = 6, 2 × 5 = 10, 2 × 7 = 14, 2 × 9 = 18 are all even; none gives an odd number. Basket: Never true. (c) \"Halving a number always leads to an even number.\" \"Give 3 examples for the statement. Can you find 3 examples when this is not true?\" Only even coconuts can be halved. Works: half of 8 is 4, half of 12 is 6, half of 20 is 10. Fails: half of 6 is 3, half of 10 is 5, half of 14 is 7. Basket: Sometimes true. A statement is done when its examples are found and the card is in the right basket; the child's own examples may differ.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2 (book Q4). Goal line: \"Tick in the appropriate cell for the following statements.\" Goal chip: \"Five statements\". Setup: a table with the rows (a) \"Adding 10 to a number gives a multiple of ten.\" (b) \"Changing the order of the numbers in subtraction makes no difference.\" (c) \"In multiplication, doubling one number and halving the other keeps the product the same.\" (d) \"Multiplication by an odd number gives an even number.\" (e) \"Multiplying a number by 5 leads to numbers which have 0 in the Ones place.\" and the columns \"Always true\", \"Sometimes true\", \"Never true\". Guess: how many are always true, \"1\", \"3\" or \"5\". Does: opens each row, tests it with the coconuts, and ticks a cell. Answers: (a) Sometimes: 10 + 10 = 20 works, 12 + 10 = 22 fails. (b) Sometimes, only for two equal numbers: 6 − 6 is the same both ways, but 8 − 3 = 5 and 3 − 8 cannot be done. (c) Always: 6 × 8 = 48 and 12 × 4 = 48. (d) Sometimes: 3 × 4 = 12 works, 3 × 5 = 15 fails. (e) Sometimes: 5 × 4 = 20 works, 5 × 3 = 15 fails.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- The child tests, counts what works and what fails, and sees that one failing example breaks \"always\", one working example breaks \"never\", and a mix means \"sometimes\". Many working examples do not prove \"always\".\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"One example that fails shows it is not always true.\" \"If no example works, it is never true.\" \"If some work and some fail, it is sometimes true.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the basket glows green, the card settles in with a gentle bounce and the goal chip turns green; the strip gives the exact line of the activity. A missed guess is never marked wrong; the true result simply plays.\n"
        "- Mistake: drops the card after fewer than 3 tests -> \"Test at least 3 numbers first.\" The rack pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: Always, but a test failed -> \"One example that fails is enough: 15 + 7 = 22 is not less than 20. It cannot be always true.\" The failed row pulses. After 3 good tests the strip also asks \"Can you find a number that fails? Try a big number.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: Never, but a test worked -> \"3 + 5 = 8 works. So it is not never true.\" The working row pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: Sometimes, but every test worked -> \"Every number you tested worked. Look for one that fails. If you cannot find one, it is always true.\" The \"Does not work\" counter pulses.\n"
        "- Hints (button \"Hint\", three levels): (1) words: \"Read the statement. Pick a small number and test it.\" (2) a visual clue: the rack glows on an odd number, an even number and a big number to test. (3) almost the answer: \"3 + 5 = 8 works and 4 + 5 = 9 fails, so some work and some fail: sometimes true.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: 1 − 1 = 0, and 0 is even; two equal numbers in subtraction; one failing example among many working ones (Adding 7); halving only even numbers; the same number used twice in a two-number test.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 is done when all three cards are in the right baskets with their examples. Star Challenge 2 is done when all five rows have the right tick.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"One example that fails shows it is not always true.\" \"If no example works, it is never true.\" \"Many examples that work do not prove it is always true.\" Think and talk: \"Can many examples that work prove that a statement is always true?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: market stall (photo); three wicker baskets with chalkboard tags (photo cut-outs); coconut with a number sticker (photo cut-out); wooden statement card and test table (code drawn); tick and cross marks (code drawn).\n"
        "- Language: English, short sentences, \"even\" and \"odd\" in words.\n"
    ),
}

OBJECTIVES = {
    "M1.S1.T1.C2.2D1": "The student sees that one array of coconuts gives a multiplication fact and two division facts, found by splitting the coconuts into groups by the columns or by the rows.",
    "M1.S7.T7.C20.2D1": "The student divides tens, hundreds and thousands using a table fact, and sees that one more zero in the number gives one more zero in the answer and that dividing by 10 slides every digit one place right.",
    "M2.S10.T10.C44.2D1": "The student divides a big number in the head by cutting it into easy parts, by rounding up and taking the extra away, or by halving again and again, and checks the answer by multiplying.",
    "M2.S15.T15.C64.2D1": "The student divides by taking away groups of the divisor, adds the groups to find the quotient, reads what is left as the remainder, and checks with N = D × Q + R.",
    "M3.S22.T22.C96.2D1": "The student divides by place value, sharing each place equally, regrouping what is left into the next place, and writing 0 where a place cannot be shared.",
    "M3.S32.T32.C150.2D1": "The student predicts how the quotient changes when the dividend or the divisor is doubled or halved, or both, and uses a known division to find related divisions.",
    "M4.S34.T34.C158.2D1": "The student finds the value of both sides of an equal sign to decide if a statement is true or false, and completes a statement so that both sides are the same.",
    "M4.S36.T36.C164.2D1": "The student tests a statement with several numbers and decides if it is always, sometimes or never true, knowing that one example that fails shows it is not always true.",
}
